A||N||9||Daleford||Spend $500 or $2000 on kitcar donor?||||5||
Z||000000||Daleford||12-30-2000||12:45 PM||||I would like some opinions:  I am building a kitcar and want to start with a Fiero that has a 5-speed Getrag. I have a Choice:<BR> Do I use a $500 86-87 chassis, or a $2000  88 chassis?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fieroparts.com||12-30-2000||12:46 PM||||If you do not plan on doing any susp. mods<BR>then go with the 88.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Oreif||12-30-2000||01:10 PM||||I'd go with the 86-87 chassis for 2 reasons.<BR>Replacement parts are less expensive and engine swaps are easier because the strut towers are slightly wider. (So I've heard)<BR>What kind of kit were you thinking about?<BR>Would it require the chassis being stretched?<BR>As for lowering it the 86-87 would be easier since the availability of dropped spindles is better.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Daleford||12-30-2000||01:37 PM||||The kit is a Testarossa with a 7" stretch.<BR>It is to be a poor man's testa, so I would not be buying many hi-tech mods, like dropped spindles. I would hope to cut coil springs, for example. <BR> Can this be done on an 88?<BR>Anybody know for sure if the 88 shock towers are narrower?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||12-30-2000||02:03 PM||||I think it depends on how you plan to use the car. I have always been satisfied with the early suspension, but I've never driven an '88. Most likely you're not going to race the Rossa, so the pre-88 suspension with polyurethane bushings and heavy swaybars should suit you fine. Take the extra $1500 you save and put it toward making the car look better.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000005||88formula||12-30-2000||02:19 PM||||Get the 88 chassis. The difference between the two is very noticeable. I had both!<P>You get what you pay for and if you go cheap you may be sorry in the end. To spend a couple grand more for a better chassis is worth it when you consider what the total cost of the project will cost you + your time and hard labor. I had the 87 chassis and didn’t like it as much as my 88. I paid an extra $2500 over the 87 and I’m very glade I did it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Daleford||12-30-2000||02:25 PM||||Looks like I'm leaning toward the 86-87 GT.  I just learned only the 88 GT was superior. The $2000. 88 donor car I located was 'only' a Formula.<BR> My car will only be for crusin'.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||12-30-2000||03:21 PM||||As far as I know, the 88 Formula and GT were pretty much the same chassis, both had rear sway bars and stiffer springs/shocks. The Formula had all the GT performance without the GT looks and luxury options.  Also, even the 88 coupes had superior geometry over the 87's. <P>Chances are the brakes and suspension will need to be rebuilt on any donor Fiero you buy, and like Oreif said, parts are cheaper and easier to find for the older ones. So if you are happy with the handling of the early chassis, there's no reason to spend the extra money for an 88. But if you want it to handle like an 88, get an 88. It's not cost effective to try to improve the early cars to the point were they are as good as an 88. Check Ryane or RCC prices to see what I mean. Only you can decide, drive both and see if you think it's worth the extra money. I personally would never pay ~$2000 more for a Fiero just because it's an 88.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||dman||12-30-2000||04:04 PM||||I agree wholeheartedly with Oreif and Rockcrawl. I've got a Testarossa project as well as a few other replicas. Ultimately it is your decision but I would personally never spend more than a 800 bucks for a donor car. Use the 1500 bucks you would save for a nicer interior, or nicer rims. But whatever you do, don't regret your decision, just build it and have fun. It'll be a blast.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||12-30-2000||10:28 PM||||I have to qualify my statement with the fact that I have never driven an 88, but it will cost less that $500 to redo the entire 86-87 suspension with bushings, ball joints and front and rear swaybars.  It is likely that the 88 would need to have bushings and balljoints replaced anyway so you really will be saving more.  Personally, I would (and am) use an 86-87 chassis and spend the difference on a 3.4L engine swap.<P>As far as a poor man's TR, I don't think you can do a Fiero stretch cheaply.  Don't forget things like fuel lines, shift and e-brake cables, and coolant lines/hoses need to be longer as well.<P>I started my F-50 project a couple of months ago, and have completed the suspension and am about a week away from putting the 3.4L engine in.  Just got back from picking up my body kit from So. Cal so I am too tired from driving to put pictures up tonight.<P>||||reg||1
